llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
122): at one token, the l2 norms of a Gated DeltaNet layer's q and k heads
run as one launch.
Generic (all GPUs): not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Single-token decode on an RX 590 pays for every dispatch as well as for the
work in it: each one costs about 4.3 us of command-processor time however
small the kernel, a 2.65 us floor plus 1.65 us for the vector-L1 invalidate
that every dispatch needs on gfx8 (docs/FINDINGS.md, "A kernel can read
stale data from the vector L1 across dispatches").

Every Gated DeltaNet layer (Qwen3.5/3.6, Qwen3-Next) normalizes the q and k
heads of its convolution output before GATED_DELTA_NET. build_gdn_l2_norm
emits each as RMS_NORM -> SCALE, which the CUDA backend already runs as one
fused rms_norm * scale kernel, so a layer launches two of them back to back:
16 rows of 128 floats each on Qwen3.6 35B-A3B, adjacent views of the same
tensor, with the same shape, strides and eps. One launch can run both: 30
dispatches per token fewer on Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (30 DeltaNet layers).

Measured on an RX 590 (Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, every expert in system RAM,
llama-bench -ncmoe 99, q8_0 KV cache): taken 30 times (once per DeltaNet
layer) per decode token, as expected; llama-perplexity at one token per
batch gives per-chunk values and summary lines equal to the build without
the patch, and greedy output (128 tokens) is byte-identical. 123-125
together: tg128 26.33 / 26.26 / 26.31 -> 26.34 / 26.67 / 26.48 tok/s in
three alternating rounds (+0.7%).

WHAT
----
ggml_cuda_try_deferred_fusion also calls ggml_cuda_try_rms_norm_scale_pair.
ggml_cuda_match_rms_norm_scale_pair matches, at an RMS_NORM, two RMS_NORM ->
SCALE pairs that ggml_cuda_can_fuse would each take for the fused
rms_norm * scale kernel, separated only by views, whose inputs are one
token of f32 rows with contiguous elements, the same shape, strides and eps,
and whose outputs are contiguous. Neither output may overlap the other pair's input or the other
output, since the second pair now runs while the first pair's input is
still being read. ggml_cuda_op_rms_norm_scale_pair launches
rms_norm_scale_pair_f32 with blockIdx.z over both tensors (2 x samples);
each block selects its tensor, output and scale and runs the body of
rms_norm_f32<block_size, false, false, true> on its row, with the block size
that rms_norm_f32_cuda picks for the row length. Everything else runs as
before, and GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_FUSION=1 turns this off with the other
fusions.

Numerics are unchanged: every block runs the single-pair kernel's
expressions in the same thread order with the same block reduction
(v_mac for the sum of squares, the same reduction tree, the division and
rsqrt, then scale_out * (scale * x)), and on gfx803 the two kernels'
floating-point instruction sequences are the same.

